---
<p>The design uses structured concurrency: <code>scope</code> blocks and <code>spawn</code> (Revision 0019, replacing earlier <code>async</code>/<code>await</code> drafts). The compiler does not run it: every section is Planned, none runnable, and nothing compiles yet.</p>
<note>Functions are ordinary functions without special declaration keywords.</note>

<h2 id="spawn">Start a task</h2>
<p><strong>Planned.</strong> <code>spawn</code> starts a child task inside a <code>scope</code> block and is valid nowhere else. The scope waits for every child, so no task outlives it. Children are canceled only on the first failure, on a <code>timeout:</code> or <code>until:</code> deadline, or by an outside cancel.</p>
<pre><code class="byn">scope {
    let task = spawn prepareIftar("Aisha")
    let meal = try task.join()
}</code></pre>
<pre><code class="other" data-lang="Go">go prepareIftar("Aisha")</code></pre>
<p>A function that can suspend must declare <code>uses suspend</code>; the compiler reports a suspension point in a function that does not.</p>

<h2 id="wait">Wait for tasks to finish</h2>
<p><strong>Planned.</strong> <code>join()</code> on a task handle waits for its result. The first child failure cancels siblings and propagates from the scope; without <code>join()</code>, the scope still waits for every child. A scope can set a deadline with <code>scope(timeout:)</code> or cap concurrency with <code>scope(limit:)</code>.</p>
<pre><code class="byn">scope(timeout: 5s) {
    let prayer = spawn checkPrayerTimes("Bilal")
    let iftar = spawn prepareIftar("Khadija")
    let p = try prayer.join()
    let m = try iftar.join()
}</code></pre>

<h2 id="channels">Send messages between tasks</h2>
<p><strong>Planned.</strong> Channels pass messages between tasks without shared mutable memory. Opening one returns a sender and receiver:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let (sender, inbox) = Channel<String>.open(capacity: 64)
scope {
    spawn deliverReminders(from: inbox)
    try sender.send("Iftar at sunset")
}</code></pre>

<h2 id="mutex">Share state with a lock</h2>
<p><strong>Planned.</strong> Shared mutable state uses <code>Mutex&lt;T&gt;</code>, <code>AsyncMutex&lt;T&gt;</code>, atomics or channels. <code>lock()</code> returns a guard; the lock releases when the guard leaves scope, or with <code>guard.unlock()</code>. Holding a <code>Mutex</code> guard across a suspend point is a compile error; use <code>AsyncMutex&lt;T&gt;</code> for those.</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let schedule = prayerSchedule.lock()
schedule.update("Umar", fajrTime)
schedule.unlock()</code></pre>
<pre><code class="other" data-lang="Go">mu.Lock()
counter++
mu.Unlock()</code></pre>
<p>Child tasks share read-only borrows without a lock; a <code>ref</code> borrow belongs to one child task at a time.</p>
